NNAAMMEE
     ppppii — user-space interface to ppbus parallel 'geek' port

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ddeevviiccee ppppii

     Minor numbering: unit numbers correspond directly to ppbus numbers.

     ##iinncclluuddee <<ddeevv//ppppbbuuss//ppppii..hh>>
     ##iinncclluuddee <<ddeevv//ppppbbuuss//ppppbbccoonnff..hh>>

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppppii driver provides a convenient means for user applications to ma‐
     nipulate the state of the parallel port, enabling easy low-speed I/O op‐
     erations without the security problems inherent with the use of the
     _/_d_e_v_/_i_o interface.

PPRROOGGRRAAMMMMIINNGG IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE
     All I/O on the ppppii interface is performed using iiooccttll() calls.  Each com‐
     mand takes a single _u_i_n_t_8___t argument, transferring one byte of data.  The
     following commands are available:

     PPIGDATA, PPISDATA
             Get and set the contents of the data register.

     PPIGSTATUS, PPISSTATUS
             Get and set the contents of the status register.

     PPIGCTRL, PPISCTRL
             Get and set the contents of the control register.  The following
             defines correspond to bits in this register.  Setting a bit in
             the control register drives the corresponding output low.
             STROBE
             AUTOFEED
             nINIT
             SELECTIN
             PCD

     PPIGEPP, PPISEPP
             Get and set the contents of the EPP control register.

     PPIGECR, PPISECR
             Get and set the contents of the ECP control register.

     PPIGFIFO, PPISFIFO
             Read and write the ECP FIFO (8-bit operations only).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     To present the value 0x5a to the data port, drive STROBE low and then
     high again, the following code fragment can be used:

             int             fd;
             uint8_t         val;

             val = 0x5a;
             ioctl(fd, PPISDATA, &val);
             ioctl(fd, PPIGCTRL, &val);
             val |= STROBE;
             ioctl(fd, PPISCTRL, &val);
             val &= ~STROBE;
             ioctl(fd, PPISCTRL, &val);


BBUUGGSS
     The inverse sense of signals is confusing.

     The iiooccttll() interface is slow, and there is no way (yet) to chain multi‐
     ple operations together.

     The headers required for user applications are not installed as part of
     the standard system.

